---
.title = "Runtime questions",
.description = "When the set of questions is only known at runtime, build a tai.Request and read a tai.Response.",
.date = .date("2026-09-26T00:00:02"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---

# When to use this

The [typed DSL]($link.page('dsl')) needs question names and choice options at
compile time. For questions built at runtime (labels loaded from a
config file, one question per retrieved passage, speculative
[fan-out](https://docs.typesafe.ai/patterns/fan-out)), use
`Client.systemOne` instead. It maps one-to-one onto the HTTP API.

```zig
const questions = try arena.alloc(tai.NamedQuestion, labels.len);
for (labels, questions) |label, *q| q.* = .{
    .name = label,
    .question = .{ .noul = .{
        .instructions = .{ .text = try std.fmt.allocPrint(arena, "Is this about {s}?", .{label}) },
    } },
};

var result = try client.systemOne(.{
    .state = .{ .text = document },
    .questions = questions,
}, .{});
defer result.deinit();

for (result.value.answers) |named| switch (named.answer) {
    .noul => |n| std.debug.print("{s}: {d:.3}\n", .{ named.name, n.noul }),
    else => {},
};
```

# Requests

```zig
pub const Request = struct {
    state: Content,
    questions: []const NamedQuestion,
    model: ?[]const u8 = null, // defaults to the client's model
};

pub const Question = union(enum) {
    noul: Noul,     // instructions, criteria: ?{ yes, no }
    choice: Choice, // instructions, options: []const { name, description: ?Content }
    score: Score,   // instructions, levels: []const Content
};
```

`tai.Content` is anything the API accepts as `string | object | array`:

| Variant | Sent as |
| --- | --- |
| `.text` | A JSON string |
| `.json` | A `std.json.Value`, as is |
| `.raw` | Pre-serialized JSON, written verbatim |

`Content.fromValue(allocator, value)` serializes any Zig value into a `.raw`.

Before anything is sent, `systemOne` checks the documented limits: at least one
question, unique names, 1 to 255 choice options with unique names, and 2 to 10
score levels. You can call `tai.validate(request)` yourself as well.

# Responses

`systemOne` returns a `std.json.Parsed(tai.Response)`. Call `deinit` to free
it.

```zig
pub const Response = struct {
    model: []const u8,
    answers: []const NamedAnswer, // in API order
    usage: Usage,

    pub fn get(name) ?Answer;
    pub fn noul(name) ?NoulAnswer;     // null if missing or another type
    pub fn choice(name) ?ChoiceAnswer;
    pub fn score(name) ?ScoreAnswer;
};
```

- `ChoiceAnswer` has `choice`, `probabilities` (option and probability pairs)
  and `confidence`, plus `probabilityOf(name)` and `as(Enum)` to convert the
  chosen option into one of your enums.
- `ScoreAnswer` has `score`, `levels` (description and probability, by index)
  and `confidence`, plus `mostLikelyLevel()`.
- Answer types this SDK version doesn't know about are kept as
  `.unknown: std.json.Value`, so a new API feature won't break your parsing.
